I've always been fascinated with the  Himalaya I spent some time there as a  kid my father used to work in the  Himalaya and it is the largest physical  feature in in India and is responsible  for the climate of North India as well  as essential for the livelihoods of a  billion people that live in the  indo-gangetic Plains due to the  ecosystem services provided by it  biodiversity species animals plants  don't recognize political borders and  they move whether the borders are there  or not what's interesting is that people  also don't recognize political borders  to the extent that we might think they  do the indigenous groups there will  often cross borders which are thought to  be extremely hostile and disputed  without really thinking too much about  it borders have become a lot less porous  than they used to be but the movement  across border still occurs both for the  flora and fauna but also for human  beings and a certain amount of trade  that still goes on the Himalaya has  often been studied for the north-south  transsex the very very sudden changes in  elevation from about sea level to 8,000  meters or more in a very very short  distance however what's equally  interesting or more interesting and what  we are looking at through the el  himalaya initiative is the east-west arc  of the east-west transit from  afghanistan and pakistan in the west to  burma in the east and along this  gradient you have a large variety of  environmental social and cultural  gradients you find the entire spectrum  of religions are most of the major  religions of the planet are well  represented in the Himalaya it's it's  it's unusual to see such a small area  with all these various religions  Christianity Buddhism Islam Hinduism  coexisting and coexisting fairly well in  many places the  is also the sort of land from where many  major rivers flow and ten major river  basins get a lot of their water from the  Himalayan region and the ecosystem  services that flow from the Himalaya  impact about a fifth of humanity over a  billion people in the indo-gangetic  Plains in the plains of Punjab and  further north in Tibet and China there  are young and growing mountain range  which is very fragile where many of the  rocks are tend to be very crumbly where  says macaque tivity is already very high  so it's it's not a very stable mountain  range such as the mountain ranges such  as the ABS for example which tend to be  an old and stable mountain range the  high population that has been living  there has resulted in the fragmentation  of the natural areas and as global  warming occurs and global warming  interestingly now seems to be occurring  faster or at a at a more accentuated  rate in the Himalaya the glacier seem to  be melting a little faster the climate  change across the slope seems to be  higher it is impacting the way the  natural flora and fauna can move  especially now that human beings have  sort of taking over patches of the  landscape and the corridors for  migration of that flora and fauna have  been greatly cut down so the Himalaya  faces threats on several fronts another  major one is the development activity we  all want electricity and so hydropower  needs to be built but is hydropower are  large dams or even run of the river dams  appropriate for the Himalaya there's a  lot of road building activity going on  without proper alignment once you  disturb a fragile rock it's often very  very hard to stabilize it and roads that  are built incorrectly will result in  landslips and landslides that go on for  years to come the Himalaya makes an  extremely interesting research area and  in some ways it's easy to study the tree  diversity for  example in the Western Himalayas  relatively low but what makes it so  interesting and complex is the interplay  of nature geology ecology forestry and  human beings unlike other forested  ecosystems in the world you don't have  large swathes of forest and then you  know human populations on the periphery  of that human beings integrate very very  well with the natural areas they are  dependent on the natural areas of these  forests and pretty much all the forests  are impacted by human beings in one way  or the other studying this interplay of  humans and nature and wildlife is  extremely interesting
 

